Documentation for mul
---------------------
rev 0.3, 2/5/99:  Added support for HalfHeight layout
rev 0.4, 2/10/99:  Corrected to support all flavors of {unsigned,signed}x{unsigned,signed}:
                   replaced SignExtendInputs with A={Signed,Unsigned,Both} and 
                   B={Signed,Unsigned,Both}
rev 0.5, 2/19/99:  Changed from TSMC25 icons to MMI generic icons
rev 0.6, 2/22/99:  Added support for NBITS_A != NBITS_B and NBITS_B != power of 2
rev 0.7, 3/9/99:   Updated to final versions of MMI generic icons
                   Added support for NBITS_A != power of 2
rev 0.8, 4/30/99:  No changes to mul.sue, just to underlying generators	
rev 0.9, 5/11/99:  No changes to mul.sue; Changed base cell names to MMIG_
rev 1.0, 5/25/99:  Added support for ReduceInputLoad parameter
rev 1.1, 5/27/99:  underlying generators changed
rev 1.2, 7/16/99:  Added NAME_mul proc, which creates the mul's name
rev 1.3, 7/27/99:  Fixed calls to underlying generators
rev 1.4, 2/29/00:  Upgraded to use generators_utilities
                   Fixed bug in icon where CSA_B_H and ASigned_H coincided
rev 1.5, 5/18/00:  Added PPG=Integrated option
rev 1.6, 5/23/00:  Added Optimize option
rev 1.7, 6/13/00:  Added support for Booth multiplication.  Changed Optimize
                   option name to OptimizeMulArray.  Changed the following 
                   option values from Y/N to 1/0:  UseMulCin, AddExtraCSA,
                   IncludePPG, IntegratePPG, ReduceInputLoad, IncludeCPA,
                   HalfHeight.  Removed bitslice option.


The multiplier generator is used to derive a multiplier made up of 4:2 adder
trees.

Three types of multiplier can be built.  They are differentiated by the type
of partial product generation logic, which also dictates the depth of the
4:2 adder trees.  The types of PPG logic are:  
  1. Simple partial product logic (one PP per multiplier bit)
  2. Booth radix 4 (roughly, one PP per 2 multiplier bits)
  3. Booth radix 8 (roughly, one PP per 3 multiplier bits)
The multiplier type selection is controlled by the PPGAlgorithm option.

Note that if IncludePPG=0, the value of PPGAlgorithm is ignored, and only
the 4:2 trees are built (from generator mmig_mul_array).

For PPGAlgorithm = Simple, the IntegratePPG option allows the user to
choose either separate PPG logic (all PPG logic is placed in front of
the 4:2 trees), or integrated PPG logic (PPG logic placed adjacent to
the appropriate 4:2 tree cell).  This option is not available for the
Booth multipliers.


NOTES ON THE CHOICE BETWEEN MULTIPLIER TYPES:
---------------------------------------------
The user should experiment with the various types of multipliers to find
the best choice for the situation.  The speed of the PPG logic is generally
ranked as follows:
  
  Simple < Booth Radix 4 < Booth Radix 8

The depth of the 4:2 trees, and therefore the speed of the 4:2 array, is
generally ranked as follows:

  Simple > Booth Radix 4 > Booth Radix 8

It should also be noted that Booth Radix 8 requires the advance generation
of 3 * multiplicand.



NOTE ON THE DISTINCTION BETWEEN THE A AND B INPUTS:
---------------------------------------------------
A is the multiplier.  B is the multiplicand.  The multiplier dictates the
number of partial products, which controls the depth of the array.  NBITS_A 
(the width of the multiplier) plus NBITS_B (the width of the multiplicand)
yields the width of the partial products, and therefore the width of the
multiplier output.

If you are multiplying two numbers of unequal width, choose the number with
the smaller width as the multiplier, in order to keep the depth of the
multiply array (and therefore its area and delay) as small as possible.


GENERATOR ARGUMENTS:
--------------------
  NBITS_A:
    Definition:  THE MULTIPLIER
      The number of bits in input value A_H.  The width of the
      multiplier's output will be NBITS_A + NBITS_B.
    Legal Values:
      1 <= NBITS_A <= 128
      Does not have to equal NBITS_B.
    Default:
      8

  NBITS_B:
    Definition:  THE MULTIPLICAND
      The number of bits in input value B_H.  The width of the
      multiplier's output will be NBITS_A + NBITS_B.
    Legal Values:
      1 <= NBITS_B <= 128
      Does not have to equal NBITS_A.
    Default:
      8

  A:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify whether the multiplier's A
      (multiplier) input is signed or unsigned.  The user can choose Signed,
      Unsigned, or Both.  If Signed is chosen, the generated multiplier will
      always treat the A input as a signed number.  If Unsigned is chosen, the
      generated multiplier will always treat the A input as an unsigned number.
      If Both is chosen, logic is included to allow the A input to be treated
      as either signed or unsigned, based on the ASigned_H control signal.
      NOTE:  there are area and speed consequences to these choices.  
        "Unsigned" is the smallest and fastest.  "Signed" is very slightly 
        larger and slower.  "Both" is noticeably larger and SIGNIFICANTLY 
        slower, and requires the use of the ASigned_H control signal which 
        drives across the width of the multiplier.  It is highly recommended 
        that you choose with care.
      NOTE:  Sign extension etc. is performed only in the PPG logic, so if
        IncludePPG = N, the value of this parameter is ignored.
      NOTE:  If Signed or Both is chosen, the msb of MCin_L is used in order
        to correctly perform a multiplication of a negative value of the
        multiplier.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Signed", "Unsigned", and "Both".
    Default:
      Unsigned
      
  B:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify whether the multiplier's B
      (multiplicand) input is signed or unsigned.  The user can choose Signed,
      Unsigned, or Both.  If Signed is chosen, the generated multiplier will
      always treat the B input as a signed number.  If Unsigned is chosen, the
      generated multiplier will always treat the B input as an unsigned number.
      If Both is chosen, logic is included to allow the B input to be treated
      as either signed or unsigned, based on the BSigned_H control signal.
      NOTE:  there are area and speed consequences to these choices.  
        "Unsigned" is the smallest and fastest.  "Signed" is very slightly 
        larger and slower.  "Both" is noticeably larger and SIGNIFICANTLY 
        slower, and requires the use of the BSigned_H control signal which 
        drives across the width of the multiplier.  It is highly recommended 
        that you choose with care.
      NOTE:  Sign extension etc. is performed only in the PPG logic, so if
        IncludePPG = N, the value of this parameter is ignored.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Signed", "Unsigned", and "Both".
    Default:
      Unsigned
      
  UseMulCin:
    Definition:
      This argument makes the multiplier's carry in bits accessible to 
      the user, or not.  If the user elects not to use the carry ins,
      they will be wired to a logic 0.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 0 and 1.
    Default:
      0

  AddExtraCSA:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify that an extra row of carry 
      save (4:2) adders be included in the multiplier array.  In addition,
      two extra input vectors (CSA_A_H and CSA_B_H), each of width NBITS_A 
      + NBITS_B, are provided as the additional inputs to this final row of 
      adders.
      This feature allows the user to implement a multiply-adder, or multiply-
      accumulator.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 0 and 1.
    Default:
      0

  OptimizeMulArray:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify various optimizations
      to the multiplier array.  
      OptimizeMulArray=None:  perform no optimizations
      OptimizeMulArray=Lsb:  remove 0-inputs to bits below the result's
        "NBITS_A" bit position.  This option may speed up the evaluation
        of the less significant result bits.
      Note that if IncludePPG = 1 and PPGAlgorithm != Simple, then
      OptimizeMulArray must have the value "Lsb".
    Legal Values:
      None, Lsb
    Default:
      None

  IncludePPG:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify whether the partial product
      generation logic is included in the multiplier.  If IncludePPG = 1,
      the type of PPG logic generated is controlled by the PPGAlgorithm
      option.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 0 and 1.
    Default:
      1

  IntegratePPG:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to select between having the PPG logic
      integrated within the multiplier array, or separate from the multiplier
      array.  Choosing IntegratePPG=1 generally results in a smaller and
      faster design.  Choosing IntegratePPG=0 may work better in some cases;
      experiment to see which is best for you.
      Note that this parameter is ignored if IncludePPG=0.
      This parameter is also ignored if PPGAlgorithm != Simple.
    Legal Values:
      0, 1
    Default:
      0

  PPGAlgorithm:
    Definition:
      This parameter allows the user to select the type of PPG logic.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Simple", "BoothRadix4", and
      "BoothRadix8".
    Default:
      Simple

  ReduceInputLoad:
    Definition:
      This argument is passed to the ppg generator, where it causes a
      reduction in the sizes of the gates which connect to the A and 
      B inputs.  This may slow down the circuits; however, if the input
      loading is too high, it may be a worthwhile tradeoff.
      NOTE:  This parameter is only used if IncludePPG = 1, PPGAlgorithm =
      Simple, and IntegratePPG = 0.
    Legal Values:
      0,1
    Default:
      0

  IncludeCPA:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify whether the carry propagate
      adder which forms the multiplier's final result is included in the
      generated schematics or not.  Omitting the carry propagate adder 
      allows the user to generate their own special purpose adder, or 
      insert registers at the multiplier tree's output.
      If the adder is included, a carry select adder of width NBITS_A +
      NBITS_B is inserted at the output of the multiplier tree.  This adder 
      is generated from the adder generator.  The adder's carry in and carry
      out signals are accessible to the user.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 1 (include carry propagate
      adder) and 0 (omit adder).
    Default:
      1

  HalfHeight:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify that the layout be done in
      (NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 bit positions, instead of (NBITS_A + NBITS_B) 
      bit positions.  Note that if (NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 is an odd number of
      bit positions, the layout will actually fit in 
      ((NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 + 1) bit positions.
      This configuration results in a slightly slower and less area 
      efficient design.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 1 (half height layout)
      0 (full height layout).
    Default:
      0



INPUT SIGNALS:
--------------
If IncludePPG = 1:
  A_H (multiplier) and B_H (multiplicand)
If IncludePPG = 0:
  PP*_H (partial product vectors)
If AddExtraCSA = 1:
  CSA_A_H, CSA_B_H (extra inputs to the final row of CSA's)
If UseMulCin = 1:
  MCin_L, MCarry_H (carry in values to multiplier array)
If IncludeCPA = 1:
  ACin_H (adder carry in)
If A = Both:
  ASigned_H
If B = Both:
  BSigned_H
If PPGAlgorithm = BoothRadix8:
  B3_H (3 * multiplicand)


OUTPUT SIGNALS:
---------------
If IncludeCPA = 1:
  Result_H (final multiply result)
  ACout_H (adder carry out)
If IncludeCPA = 0:
  Sum_H, Carry_H (multiply array redundant outputs)


